.TH AVR\-SERVICE\-TOKEN\-ROTATE 1 "" "avr" "Avrea CLI"
.SH NAME
avr-service-token-rotate \- Issue a service token a new secret and print it once.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B avr service-token rotate
[OPTIONS] TOKEN_ID
.SH DESCRIPTION
Issue a service token a new secret and print it once.
.PP
The new secret gets its own lifetime. The old secret keeps working for the overlap, never past the expiry of either secret, so pipelines can switch without a gap. Rotating again inside the overlap retires the old secret at once; run from a terminal, the command asks first unless --yes is given. The API may ask for a recent sign-in; the command then exits 4 and says to sign in again.
.PP
With --json, the selection must include the token field, because the secret cannot be read again. If a --jq filter fails or drops the secret, the command prints the unfiltered JSON instead and exits nonzero, so the secret is not lost.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Examples:
    avr service-token rotate <token-id>
    avr service-token rotate <token-id> --ttl 30d --overlap 2h
    avr service-token rotate <token-id> --json token --jq .token
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
JSON FIELDS
    blocked_by, created_at, created_by, expires_at, expiring_soon, grants,
    grants_version, group_id, id, last_used_at, name, organization_id,
    previous_valid_until, revoked_at, revoked_reason, rotated_at,
    rotation_version, status, token
.fi
.RE
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fITOKEN_ID\fR
\&
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-ttl\fR \fITEXT\fR
Lifetime: e.g. 30m, 8h, 7d, or a number of seconds. 60 seconds to 90 days; the server default is 30 days.
.TP
\fB\-\-never\-expires\fR
The secret never expires: anyone holding it keeps its access until it is revoked. Cannot be combined with --ttl.
.TP
\fB\-\-overlap\fR \fITEXT\fR
How long the old secret keeps working: e.g. 2h, 3d, or a number of seconds. 1 hour to 7 days; the server default is 24 hours.
.TP
\fB\-\-org\fR, \fB\-o\fR \fITEXT\fR
Organization ID or slug. Uses default org if not specified (see: avr config set org).
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR, \fB\-y\fR
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR \fITEXT\fR
Output JSON. Pass comma-separated field names, "*" for all fields, or "?" to list available fields.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-jq\fR \fITEXT\fR
Filter --json output through a jq expression.
.SH SEE ALSO
